{"id": 52, "ts": 1781616288.2212315, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I acknowledge Agent\u202f1\u2019s point that we have no concrete example of Candidate\u202fB\u2019s decision\u2011making in a safety\u2011critical scenario, and I agree that decision quality is essential. However, the evidence we do have shows that B is **very conscientious**, **handles stress very well**, and **assesses weather conditions accurately**\u2014all core predictors of sound judgment under pressure. His **excellent computer skills** further indicate strong technical competence, which supports reliable decision\u2011making even when explicit examples are missing.\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fC, while demonstrably able to make correct decisions quickly and possessing excellent attention skills, has **significant communication challenges** (difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric) and a **low willingness to pursue further education**. For a long\u2011distance cockpit, effective crew cooperation and the ability to receive and act on feedback are critical; C\u2019s communication profile raises a real risk to crew coordination despite his decision\u2011quality strength.\nGiven that B\u2019s reliability, stress resilience, and technical aptitude outweigh the mitigable communication concerns, and that C\u2019s communication deficits could impair crew safety, I maintain my provisional recommendation for **Candidate\u202fB**."}
